    shape most apps land on, and it's the hardest to change later because everything downstream assumes it.
  - [1mAn existing auth system already provides it[0m — there's a session, SSO, or backend elsewhere that hands us an ID
    before the login form is even reached. Buys you a simple always-present parameter and the cleanest signature. Costs:
    only viable if that system exists — I don't see one in this repo, so you'd have to point me at it.
  - [1mWe mint and own it client-side[0m — the app generates a stable ID on first visit, persists it, and passes it everywhere
    including the first login. Buys you something that works today with no backend, uniform across all forms. Costs: it
    identifies a browser, not a person. It survives logout and is shared by everyone on that device, which makes "who
    logged in" only approximately true.

  These are not equally reversible. The first two are real identity and can be swapped for each other with moderate
  work; the third is a different meaning of "user" and migrating off it later means reconciling browser IDs with account
  IDs after the fact.

  [1mMy recommendation: server issues it at login.[0m It's the only option that actually answers "track who logged in" with an
  ID that means a person, and it doesn't require infrastructure you'd have to invent. I'd rather absorb the
  optional-on-first-call wrinkle in the signature than build tracking on an ID that can't distinguish two people sharing
  a laptop.

  One thing I'll carry into the design regardless of your answer: persisting a user identifier in the browser is a
  security and privacy decision, not just a storage choice — where it lives, how long it survives, and whether logout
  clears it. I'll put that in its own design section rather than picking silently.
